dict(id="c1-translator", level="C1", title="The Untranslatable", tr="Çevrilemeyen", text="""
Nilay Kaya had translated forty novels into Turkish, yet the word that kept her awake that winter was a single, unremarkable adjective. The novel she was working on, a sprawling family saga by a celebrated Scottish author, described the weather as 'dreich', a Scots term for days that are simultaneously grey, damp, cold and dismal. In the original, the word carried both the gloom of the landscape and a hint of dry humour; every Turkish equivalent she tried captured one quality {at the expense of} the other.

Translation, she often told her students, is less a science than a series of compromises. A literal rendering may be accurate yet lifeless, while a free one risks betraying the intentions of the author. The skill lies in deciding, sentence by sentence, which losses are acceptable. Some translators add footnotes; Nilay considered them an admission of defeat, a way of interrupting the reader to explain a joke.

Determined to find a solution, she did something she had not done in years: she travelled to the place the novel described. For a week she walked along the coast of north-east Scotland, sheltering from relentless drizzle and listening to the way people spoke in cafés and on buses. She noticed that locals used the word almost affectionately, as though complaining about the weather were a form of companionship.

That observation changed her approach. Instead of searching for a single word, she rewrote the passage so that the mood of the heroine and the landscape mirrored each other, borrowing an expression her grandmother had used about the drizzly autumns of the Black Sea coast. The sentence was longer than the original and contained no direct equivalent of 'dreich', yet when she read it aloud it produced the same wry melancholy.

When the translation was published, a critic praised it as more faithful in spirit than in letter, a phrase Nilay chose to take as a compliment. Some readers, however, wrote to complain that she had {taken liberties} with the text, and one even compiled a list of words she had failed to translate. She replied to every letter. Translation, she wrote, is not a matter of replacing words but of recreating experiences, and no two languages {divide up} the world in exactly the same way.

The episode left her with a conviction she still repeats to her students: the untranslatable word is rarely the real problem. The real challenge is understanding a culture well enough to know what the word is doing.
"""),
dict(id="c1-restorer", level="C1", title="Beneath the Surface", tr="Yüzeyin Altında", text="""
When a small regional museum asked Marta Lindqvist to clean a dull seventeenth-century landscape, she expected a routine job. The painting had hung in a dim corridor for decades, its colours hidden beneath layers of yellowed varnish and the soot of countless winters. Restoration of this kind is painstaking: solvents must be tested on tiny areas, and a careless moment can remove original paint along with centuries of dirt.

On the third day, while examining the canvas under ultraviolet light, Marta noticed something odd. Retouched areas usually appear as dark patches under ultraviolet light, but here an entire section of the sky looked suspiciously uniform. An infrared scan confirmed her suspicion: beneath the clouds lay the faint outline of a figure, a woman standing at the edge of the water, deliberately painted over.

The discovery posed a dilemma that lies at the heart of conservation ethics. Removing the overpaint might reveal the original intention of the artist; equally, it might destroy a later alteration that had become part of the history of the painting. Perhaps the artist himself had changed his mind. Perhaps a nineteenth-century owner had found the figure unfashionable. Each possibility implied a different responsibility.

Marta spent weeks on research before touching the surface again. Chemical analysis of tiny samples showed that the paint covering the figure contained a pigment that had not been manufactured until roughly a hundred and fifty years after the picture was completed. The overpaint, {in other words}, could not be the work of the artist. Archive records added a further clue: an inventory made around 1885 described the painting as a landscape with a solitary figure, while every later document mentioned only a river and sky.

With the agreement of the museum, she removed the later layer millimetre by millimetre, a process that took almost four months. The figure that emerged was small but striking, a woman shielding her eyes as she gazed across the river, and her presence transformed the mood of the entire composition from tranquil to expectant.

When the painting returned to the gallery, it was hung in the main hall, accompanied by photographs documenting each stage of the work. Visitor numbers that year rose noticeably, although Marta insists that the real achievement was not the discovery itself but the restraint that preceded it. "The temptation is always to reveal," she says. "Sometimes the most responsible decision is to wait until you are certain."
"""),
dict(id="c1-lighthouse", level="C1", title="The Last Keeper", tr="Son Fener Bekçisi", text="""
For almost thirty years, the day of Thomas Brennan began at dusk. As keeper of a lighthouse on a rocky island off the west coast of Ireland, he climbed the spiral staircase each evening to light the lamp, wound the clockwork that turned the lens, and recorded the weather every three hours in a leather-bound log. Supplies arrived by boat once a fortnight when the sea allowed, and during winter storms he could go for over a month without speaking to another person.

Then, towards the end of the twentieth century, a letter arrived informing him that the light was to be automated. Electric lamps, solar panels and remote monitoring would make his position unnecessary. Thomas had anticipated the news for years, since lighthouse after lighthouse along the coast had already been converted, yet reading it still felt like learning of a death in the family.

Automation brought undeniable advantages. Modern systems were cheaper to operate, rarely failed, and removed the need for anyone to live in conditions that were often dangerous. Satellite navigation, meanwhile, had made ships far less dependent on lights for safe passage. From the perspective of the authorities, the decision was simply rational.

What the official reports could not measure was what would be lost. Keepers had done far more than maintain a lamp. They had rescued stranded fishermen, reported vessels in difficulty, and kept detailed weather records that scientists now value as an unbroken source of climate data stretching back more than a century. Thomas also knew the seabird colonies of the island better than any researcher, having counted nesting puffins every spring out of personal curiosity.

On his final night, he lit the lamp by hand one last time, although the new system had been installed and tested weeks earlier. The following morning a helicopter took him to the mainland, where he moved into a small house within sight of the sea.

Retirement did not suit him at first. He found the constant noise of traffic exhausting and missed the discipline of the night watch. Gradually, however, he found a new role. He began giving talks at schools and maritime museums, and he donated his logbooks to a university, where researchers used his records of storms and seabird numbers to study changes in the North Atlantic.

"People think the job was about loneliness," he says. "It was really about {paying attention}. A machine can keep a light burning, but it cannot notice that the puffins are arriving later every year."
"""),
dict(id="c1-radiologist", level="C1", title="A Second Opinion", tr="İkinci Görüş", text="""
Leila Haddad had been reading chest scans for fifteen years when her hospital introduced an artificial intelligence system designed to detect early signs of lung cancer. The software had been trained on millions of images and, according to the manufacturer, matched the accuracy of experienced specialists. Within a month it was flagging suspicious shadows on scans before Leila had even opened them.

Her initial reaction was a mixture of curiosity and unease. The system was undeniably fast, and on several occasions it identified tiny nodules that she might easily have overlooked at the end of a long shift. Yet it also produced false alarms, highlighting harmless scars as potential tumours, and it could not explain why it had reached a particular conclusion. When a patient asked her why a further test was needed, "the computer thinks so" felt like an unacceptable answer.

The tension {came to a head} with a scan belonging to a sixty-year-old former smoker. The software rated the risk as low. Leila, however, was troubled by a faint irregularity that the algorithm had apparently dismissed. She requested an additional scan despite pressure to reduce costs, and a biopsy later confirmed an early-stage cancer, small enough to be treated successfully.

The case could easily be told as a story of human intuition triumphing over machines, but Leila resists that interpretation. In the same year, she points out, the software caught at least three cancers that she and her colleagues had initially missed. The lesson, in her view, is not that one is superior to the other but that each fails in different ways. Human doctors become tired and distracted; algorithms reproduce the limitations of the data on which they were trained, and may perform poorly for groups of patients who were underrepresented in that data.

Researchers increasingly describe the most effective approach as collaboration {rather than} replacement. In several studies, radiologists working together with artificial intelligence have outperformed either working alone, provided that the doctors remain willing to question the software {rather than} deferring to it automatically. This last condition is harder to meet than it sounds. Psychologists have long documented automation bias, the tendency to trust automated systems even when they are wrong.

Leila now teaches a course for junior doctors on working with diagnostic software. Her first lesson is always the same: treat the algorithm as a highly knowledgeable but occasionally overconfident colleague. "You would never accept the opinion of a colleague without understanding it," she tells them. "Do not make an exception just because this one never gets tired."
"""),
dict(id="c1-glacier", level="C1", title="The Shrinking Ice", tr="Eriyen Buz", text="""
When Andreas Huber guided his first group across the glacier above his village in the Austrian Alps, the ice began just a short walk from the mountain hut. Thirty years later, the same route requires an hour of climbing over loose rock before clients even reach the ice, and the owner of the hut has installed metal ladders to help walkers descend the steep slopes that the glacier once covered.

Alpine glaciers have been retreating since the middle of the nineteenth century, but the pace has accelerated sharply in recent decades. Scientists estimate that glaciers in the Alps have lost more than half of their volume since that time, and many smaller glaciers are expected to disappear entirely within the lifetime of children alive today. For mountain guides, these figures are not abstractions but a daily reality: crevasses open in unexpected places, rockfalls become more frequent as frozen ground thaws, and routes that were considered safe for generations must be abandoned.

Andreas has adapted his work accordingly. He now starts tours earlier in the morning, when the ice is firmer, and he has removed several classic routes from his programme altogether. He also spends more time explaining to clients why the mountains look the way they do. Many visitors, he says, arrive expecting the white landscapes of old photographs and are shocked by the grey rubble that has replaced them.

These conversations have changed the nature of his job. Some colleagues worry that talking about climate change spoils the holidays of their clients, but Andreas has found that most people want to understand what they are seeing. A few years ago he began collaborating with researchers from a university in Innsbruck, photographing the glacier from fixed points every summer. The resulting images, placed side by side, show the retreat more powerfully than any graph.

Not everyone in his village shares his concerns. Tourism remains the main source of income, and some residents fear that emphasising decline will discourage visitors. Others argue that the region should invest in new attractions, such as mountain biking, that depend less on snow and ice. Andreas understands both positions, though he suspects that honesty will prove more sustainable than denial.

His own children have chosen different careers, one as an engineer and the other as a teacher, and he is not sure he would encourage them to become guides. "I was lucky," he says. "I worked on the ice while it was still there. The next generation will be guiding people through the place where it used to be."
"""),
